  "captions": {
    "noiseless": "Noiseless camera responses of the BEC, SSP and ID density profiles in PCI, DGI, DFFI and DPFI. PCI and DGI show blank-subtracted intensity, DFFI shows the crossed-polariser intensity, and DPFI shows the difference between the H and V analyser ports. Each quantity is divided by the same incident intensity I0, and all twelve panels share one zero-centred colour scale. Colour therefore compares the fractional detected response directly, including the optical gain of each readout, but does not compare noise or measurement precision. The signed DGI and DPFI responses are shown without clipping. In the ID profile, the phase of the stronger circular component exceeds pi locally; the forward images remain valid, but their intensity cannot be inverted globally to give a unique column density.",
    "noisy": "One 200-microsecond exposure of each BEC, SSP and ID density profile with PCI, DGI, DFFI and DPFI. Every panel uses the same incident probe dose and detector model, including photon shot noise and 0.7-electron read noise. DPFI is formed from two independently noisy H and V analyser ports, so subtracting their mean backgrounds does not remove their shot or read noise. All twelve panels are divided by the same incident intensity I0 and share one zero-centred colour scale, allowing their fractional detected responses to be compared directly. In the ID profile, the stronger circular phase exceeds pi locally, so image strength is not a globally single-valued measure of column density.",
    "snr": "Expected whole-image SNR versus pulse duration under the same ideal readout and detector model. The dashed line marks the 200-microsecond acquisition shown above. This statistic measures aggregate image strength; it is not a resolution, feature-precision or state-classification probability. The PCI BEC and ID curves nearly coincide; alternating markers keep both visible without changing the data. The Faraday results assume matched ideal analyser ports; uncalibrated extinction, gain imbalance and port registration are outside this comparison."
  },
